 ],
 "literal": "Women with fine ornaments on forearms with rows of bangles / having spread red millet on wide rock-ledges of dark stone, / watching for the moment of inattention when they plunge into mountain pools, descending from branches / green-eyed female monkeys with their young ones snatch [the millet] / in the mountain, indeed near is [the village], of the younger sister of foresters with strong bows and long shafts, / the mountain girl with broad shoulders, the village where she lived.",
 "translation": "The women with fine ornaments on forearms adorned with rows of bangles spread red millet to dry on the wide rock-ledges of the dark mountain. Watching for their moment of inattention when they plunge into the mountain pools, green-eyed female monkeys with their young ones descend from the branches and snatch the grain. In this very mountain lies the village where she lives—the broad-shouldered mountain girl, the younger sister of the foresters who carry strong bows and long arrows.",
 "modern": "வரிசையான வளையல்களை அணிந்த முன்கைகளையும் சிறந்த அணிகலன்களையும் உடைய பெண்கள், கரிய பெரிய பாறையின் அகன்ற பரப்பில் செந்தினையைக் காயவைத்துப் பரப்பினர். அவர்கள் சுனைநீரில் குதித்து விளையாடும் கவனக்குறைவான நேரத்தைப் பார்த்து, பசிய கண்களையுடைய பெண் குரங்கு தன் குட்டியோடு மரக்கிளையிலிருந்து இறங்கி வந்து அத்தினையைக் கவர்ந்து செல்லும். அத்தகைய மலையிடத்தே உள்ளது, நீண்ட அம்புகளையும் வலிமையான வில்லையும் உடைய கானவர்களின் தங்கையான, பருத்த தோள்களைக் கொண்ட குறிஞ்சி நிலப் பெண்ணாகிய தலைவி வாழும் ஊர்.",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) speaks to the hero (talaivaṉ) who has been coming for secret trysts, subtly urging him to seek a formal marriage. She describes the mountain landscape where the heroine lives, pointing out that her village is very close. The image of the green-eyed female monkey watching for a moment of distraction to steal the millet represents how others (like the heroine's family or villagers) might discover their secret love if they are careless. The mention of her brothers as foresters with strong bows and long arrows serves as a gentle warning of the dangers of delaying the marriage. This rich imagery of the mountain slopes, wild monkeys, and foresters perfectly aligns with the Kuriñci tiṇai (mountain landscape), which is the setting for union and secret meetings.",
